Below is the exact Windows CMD workflow you need for your folder:

Code
C:\ces
No guessing, no PATH editing.

✅ Step 1 — Create the venv inside C:\ces
Open CMD:

Code
cd C:\ces
py -3.10 -m venv venv
This creates:

Code
C:\ces\venv\
✅ Step 2 — Activate the venv (CMD)
Code
C:\ces\venv\Scripts\activate
After activation, your prompt will look like:

Code
(venv) C:\ces>
This means the environment is active.

✅ Step 3 — Install dependencies inside the venv
✔ Install Oracle OLE DB support (win32com)
Code
pip install pywin32
✔ Install SQL Server ODBC support
Code
pip install pyodbc
✔ (Optional) Install cx_Oracle
You only need this if you switch to Oracle Instant Client later.

Code
pip install cx_Oracle
🧠 Why pywin32 is required
Oracle OLE DB provider MSDAORA.1 is COM‑based, so Python must use:

python
win32com.client.Dispatch("ADODB.Connection")
That requires pywin32.

🎯 Step 4 — Verify both dependencies installed
Code
pip list
You should see:

Code
pywin32
pyodbc
cx_Oracle (optional)
🎯 Step 5 — Run your Python script inside the venv
Code
python yourscript.py
or explicitly:

Code
py -3.10 yourscript.py
Both work because the venv is active.
